2.text:
For the family

\begin{equation*}
y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}\qquad  E>0 ,
\end{equation*}
put $\omega_{k}=q^{k}y^{-1}dq$.  On the invariant subspace
$x\mapsto -x$ the $d/dE$–action closes on the $k$ even monomials
$1,q^{2},\ldots ,q^{\,n-2}$.  Hence, up to the trivial scaling
solution, the even period

\begin{equation*}
\Pi_{e}(E)=\oint_{e}y\,dq
\end{equation*}
satisfies a Picard–Fuchs equation of

\begin{equation*}
r_{e}(n)=\#\{k\;{\rm even}:0\le k\le n-2\}-1
       =\frac n2-1\qquad n\;{\rm even}
\end{equation*}
and on the odd subspace
$r_{o}(n)=n-1$ for $n$ odd.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\Big[\frac{\,n-1\,}{2}\Big],\qquad 
u_{r}(2k)=k-1,\qquad u_{r}(2k+1)=k ,
\end{equation*}
so that $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ is the reduced even order and
$u_{r}(n)=n-1$ on the odd subspace.  Hence on the even branch

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2k)=k-1,\qquad u_{r}(2k\pm1)=2k\pm1-1,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  the reduced order is $n/2-1$ and the two odd neighbours are
generic.  Modulo the trivial $n=2$ free particle the first
non–trivial reduced orders are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual convention $n_{1}=2$, $n_{2}=4$ the free particle
is counted.  In the non–trivial normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{12}:=\frac{n_{1}-1}{\,n_{2}\,}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12 ,
\end{equation*}
and up to the conventional $2\pi$–normalisation the even period is
constant on the quadratic branch.  Writing the Hamiltonian in the
normal form

\begin{equation*}
 H_{\lambda}(p,q)=\frac12\Big(p^{2}+q^{2}-\lambda q^{m}\Big), 
\qquad m=\frac{n_{1}}2,
\end{equation*}
the (real) period is

\begin{equation*}
T_{\lambda}(E)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-\lambda\,}}\,,
\qquad 
\lambda_{12}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}
\sqrt{\frac{\,n_{2}-n_{1}\,}{n_{1}/2}}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the $n_{1}=4$ branch $m=2$ and $H_{\lambda_{12}}$ is the
harmonic oscillator of frequency $\omega_{12}=\sqrt{1-\lambda_{12}}
=1/\sqrt2$.  With the usual $T(0)=2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{\lambda_{12}}= \frac{2\pi}{\omega_{12}}=2\sqrt2\,\pi ,
\end{equation*}
and with the normalised hypergeometric period $T_{0}(0)=1$,
$T_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\lambda_{12}\alpha)^{-1/2}$, we obtain the
normalised value

\begin{equation*}
T_{0}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)=
T_{0}\!\left(\frac12\right)=\frac1{\sqrt{\,1-\lambda_{12}/2\,}}
=\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $2\pi\sqrt2$ in the un–normalised physical period.  In
particular the period is $\alpha$–independent on the $m=2$ branch;
with the conventional $2\pi$ normalisation this is the familiar
$T\equiv2\pi/\sqrt{1-\lambda}$.

Remark.  Counting the trivial free particle $n=2$ the first two
even reduced orders are $n_{1}=2$, $n_{2}=4$ and
$\alpha_{12}=(n_{1}-1)/n_{2}=1/4$.  On the $n_{1}=2$ branch $m=1$ the
Hamiltonian is the shifted harmonic oscillator
$H= \tfrac12\big(p^{2}+q^{2}-\sqrt2\,q\big)$, whose (normalised)
real period is the constant $T\equiv1$, i.e. $T_{phys}\equiv2\pi$,
the usual statement that the hypergeometric period is constant on
the quadratic branch.  This is the $n_{1}=4$, $n_{2}=6$ statement
above written with the $2\pi$–normalisation $T_{phys}=2\sqrt2\pi$,
$T_{0}=\sqrt2$, which is the customary $\alpha$–independent value
at $\alpha=1/2$. ∎

