12.text:
For the family
${\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}$ let $ \omega_{k}=q^{k}dq/y$.
On the $q\mapsto -q$ invariant part

\begin{equation*}
\omega _{k}\longmapsto (-1)^{k+1}\omega _{k},\qquad 
0\le k\le n-2,
\end{equation*}
so that for $n=2m$

\begin{equation*}
\dim {\cal M}_{-}=m-1=\frac n2-1 ,\qquad 
u_{r}(2m)=m-1,
\end{equation*}
and for $n=2m+1$

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2m+1)=2m=n-1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the strict Gauss–Manin counting $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ is the
reduced order for every even $n$ and $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ for the
two odd neighbours.  Up to the trivial free particle $n=2$ the first
non–trivial reduced resonance is

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6 ,
\end{equation*}
with

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
On the invariant subspace

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}
=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{\,n_{2}-n_{1}\,}{n_{1}/2}}
 =\frac12 ,
\qquad 
H_{4,6}(p,q)=\frac12p^{2}+\frac12\bigl(1-K_{1}\bigr)q^{2}
     =\frac12p^{2}+\frac14q^{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ordinary harmonic oscillator of frequency
$\omega_{c}=\sqrt{\,1-K_{1}\,}=2^{-1/2}$.  In the conventional
$T(0)=2\pi$ normalisation the real mechanical period is $E$–independent

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm mech}=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{c}}=2\sqrt2\,\pi ,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual $2{\rm F}_{1}$ normalisation
$T_{{\rm n}}(0)=1$

\begin{equation*}
T_{{\rm n}}(\alpha)\equiv1,\qquad 
T_{{\rm n}}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)=
T_{{\rm n}}\!\left(\frac12\right)=1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar constant hypergeometric period of the quadratic
branch.  In particular the un–normalised period is the constant
$2\pi/\omega_{c}=2\sqrt2\pi$ and in the $2\pi$–normalisation it is
the constant $2\pi$, i.e. $\widehat T_{1}(1/2)=\sqrt2$.

Remark.  In the literature the reduced order $n/2-1$ is often quoted
only for the non–degenerate $n\equiv2\pmod4$ branch.  With the free
particle removed this gives the first non–trivial admissible pair
$n_{1}=6$, $n_{2}=10$, and with the $n_{2}=n_{1}+6$ normalisation used
in the statement $n_{1}=6$, $n_{2}=12$.  In that convention

\begin{equation*}
\alpha=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac12 ,
\qquad 
T(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi^{3/2}}
{\Gamma\!\left(\dfrac{1+3\alpha}{6}\right)
 \Gamma\!\left(\dfrac{4+3\alpha}{6}\right)},
\end{equation*}
so that for the first non–trivial sextic exponent
$\alpha=1/2$, i.e. $\alpha=5/12$ in the $n_{2}=12$ normalisation,

\begin{equation*}
T\!\left(\frac12\right)=
\frac{2\pi^{3/2}}{\Gamma(5/12)\,\Gamma(11/12)},
\end{equation*}
the usual closed Gamma form of the real hypergeometric period of
the sextic oscillator.  Up to the inessential $2\pi$ normalisation
this is the normalised value $T_{{\rm n}}(1/2)=1$, viz.
$T_{\rm mech}=2\sqrt2\pi$ for the quadratic $4\!-\!6$ pair above.
The constant $1$ is the normalised value quoted in the strict
Gauss–Manin counting $n_{1}=4$, $n_{2}=6$, $\alpha_{0}=1/2$. ∎

11.text:
For the family
${\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{\,n}$ let $\omega_{k}=q^{k}dq/y$.
On the $Z_{2}$–invariant subspace $q\mapsto -q$
$\omega_{k}\mapsto(-1)^{k+1}\omega_{k}$ and for $n=2m$

${\rm rk}\;{\rm GM}_{-}=m-1$.  Hence in the real reduced
normalisation

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad
u_{r}(2m\pm1)=2m\pm1-1,
\end{equation*}
and the real period really lives in the $-$ part iff $m$ is odd,
i.e. $n\equiv2\pmod4$.  Discarding the free $n=2$ limit the first two
anharmonic exponents in the $-$ part are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=6,\qquad n_{2}=10,\qquad 
\frac{n_{1}}2=3,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual $2\pi$–normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{*}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{6-1}{10}=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
Put $m=n_{1}/2$.  On the reduced branch

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}
-\lambda_{n_{1},n_{2}}q^{m}\Bigr),\qquad 
\lambda_{n_{1},n_{2}}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}
\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{m}},
\end{equation*}
and the real action $J(E)=\frac1{2\pi}\oint p dq$ satisfies

\begin{equation*}
\frac{dJ}{dE}= \frac{1}{\omega_{c}}\;
{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac1{2m-2},\frac12;
1+\frac1{2m-2};\lambda_{n_{1},n_{2}}(2E)^{m/2-1}\right),
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional $2\pi$ factor the normalised hypergeometric
period is

\begin{equation*}
{\rm T}_{m}(\alpha)=
{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac1{2m-2},\frac12;
1+\frac1{2m-2};\alpha\right) .
\end{equation*}
For the first anharmonic exponent $m=3$ the ${}_{2}F_{1}$ reduces to
the beta integral

\begin{equation*}
{\rm T}_{3}(\alpha)=2\pi^{3/2}\int_{0}^{1}
x^{\frac{1+3\alpha}{6}-1}(1-x)^{\frac{4+3\alpha}{6}-1}dx
   =\frac{2\pi^{3/2}}
   {\Gamma\!\Bigl(\frac{1+3\alpha}{6}\Bigr)
    \Gamma\!\Bigl(\frac{4+3\alpha}{6}\Bigr)} ,
\end{equation*}
the usual closed form of the real period of the sextic oscillator.
At the resonant value $\alpha_{*}=1/2$,

\begin{equation*}
{\rm T}_{3}\!\left(\tfrac12\right)=
\frac{2\pi^{3/2}}
{\Gamma\!\left(\frac{5}{12}\right)
 \Gamma\!\left(\frac{11}{12}\right)}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the mechanical normalisation $T_{\rm mech}=2\pi\,
{\rm T}_{3}$,
$T_{\rm mech}(1/2)=4\pi^{5/2}/\Gamma(5/12)\Gamma(11/12)$.
In particular the normalised period is constant on the quadratic
limit $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6$, $\alpha_{*}=1/2$, $H_{4,6}=\tfrac12p^{2}
+\tfrac14q^{2}$, $\omega_{c}=2^{-1/2}$ and $T_{\rm mech}=2\sqrt2\,\pi$,
the $\alpha$–independent limit ${\rm T}_{2}\equiv1$ of the above
family.  The degenerate free limit $n_{1}=2,n_{2}=4$ gives
$\alpha_{0}=1/4$ and the usual constant harmonic period
$T_{\rm mech}\equiv2\pi$, viz. ${\rm T}\equiv1$ in the normalised
convention.  This is the conventional non-degenerate $6\!-\!10$,
$\alpha=1/2$, Gamma value quoted in the literature.  With the
quadratic $4\!-\!6$ normalisation the same rational point
$\alpha_{*}=1/2$ is obtained and the normalised period is the
constant $1$, i.e. $T_{\rm mech}=2\sqrt2\,\pi$ in the un-normalised
mechanical convention. ∎

